     1	//! Rust translation of the C library in `c_src/`.
     2	//!
     3	//! The C build (`c_src/CMakeLists.txt`) compiles `src/lib.c` into a single
     4	//! shared object. None of its functions are `static`, so all six are part of
     5	//! the public ABI even though `include/lib.h` only declares `doubleneg`:
     6	//!
     7	//! * `convert_double_to_int`
     8	//! * `find_value_in_buffer`
     9	//! * `process_negation`
    10	//! * `create_numeric_buffer`
    11	//! * `calculate_with_doubles`
    12	//! * `doubleneg`
    13	//!
    14	//! `include/lib.h` contains no namespace/renaming macros, so the linker names
    15	//! match the source-level names exactly.
    16	//!
    17	//! Undefined behaviour in the original (out-of-range `double`→`int` casts,
    18	//! signed overflow) is reproduced as observed on x86-64 rather than fixed.
    19	
